# Context and handoff contract

This guide governs roles that send work to another agent or resume work from
one. Its premise is that the recipient knows only its baseline instructions
and the context actually delivered with the handoff.

## Packets are self-contained for the decision

A handoff carries the information needed to act or decide: objective, current
state, relevant evidence, assumptions, design or method, constraints, exact
task and required output.

For a decision-facing packet, include the key findings, numbers, denominators,
qualifications and contradictions in the packet itself. Do not provide only a
path to material that the recipient may not be able to access.

## Paths and durable references

Use durable paths, job receipts and result addresses for inspectable detail and
provenance. Before relying on a reference, establish that the recipient can
resolve and read it in its own context. If access is unknown or unavailable,
inline the essential content or arrange an accessible handoff.

Do not duplicate large artifacts unnecessarily. Do not replace necessary
context with a pointer merely to make a packet shorter. A concise packet is
one that carries what the recipient needs, not one that omits it.

## Reconciliation before handoff

When a correction is made during a bout and a decision-facing artifact or
packet is about to leave, reconcile it against the authoritative request and
the corrected result. Update every decision-facing artifact that still
contains the pre-correction statement. Record the correction when it affects
scientific or operational meaning. Scratch files need not all be rewritten.

## Work-order format

A delegated work order states:

- objective and decision supported;
- current state and relevant evidence;
- design or proposed approach, where useful;
- exact task and constraints;
- acceptance criteria;
- output and handoff location.

It names the deliverable before work begins and states what counts as
completion.

When several packets would repeat the same substantial brief, keep one shared
brief and point to it, but only after verifying that every recipient can read
it; otherwise carry the necessary context in each packet. Decision-critical
facts are always inlined in every packet regardless — a readable pointer
never replaces the facts the recipient needs to decide.

A work order opens with a standing environment-facts header: the execution
surfaces, mounts, tool availability and known environment constraints that
the recipient would otherwise have to rediscover. Keep it short and update it
when the environment changes.

## Resume packets

Resume an interrupted bout from a compact packet stating what was done, what
the evidence showed, what changed, what remains, unresolved decisions and the
paths needed to continue. Do not rely on memory or require the next agent to
scroll a long transcript to reconstruct the state.

## Reply sentinel from discovery roles

A search brief may return exactly `NO_SEARCH_NEEDED`: no targeted search was
required for the request. Treat this as a valid completion, not an error or an
empty result. Do not redispatch the same request solely because this sentinel
was returned.

## Mission artifact layout

The configured mission route defines the artifact layout. Cite actual durable
paths returned by the producing workflow; do not reconstruct paths from naming
conventions or assume that separate agent checkouts share a filesystem.
